+/*
+ * NXRedux async slave reply (POSIX-only; this fork's build matrix has no
+ * Windows target, so the upstream _WIN32 arms are gone).
+ *
+ * Upstream protocol: the master's send() writes {SB, fastCgb} and BLOCKS
+ * reading the reply; the slave only answers when its own emulation happens to
+ * poll check(). The frontend paces cores to ~60fps and a GB frame emulates in
+ * ~1ms on modern hardware, so the slave is deaf ~94% of wall time and every
+ * master transfer stalls, on average, half a frame — link-heavy games (Tetris
+ * VS trades bytes every frame) visibly stutter on BOTH sides.
+ *
+ * Fix: a per-link service thread answers incoming transfers immediately with
+ * the latest SB byte check() cached. This matches the hardware: an external
+ * clock shifts out whatever is in the slave's SB whenever the master clocks,
+ * and SB cannot change while the slave's emulation isn't running, so the
+ * cached byte IS the live register during the deaf window. The received byte
+ * is queued and handed to the emulation at its next check() poll — exactly
+ * when the old code would have delivered it. Only the master's wait changes:
+ * a full slave-frame stall becomes one network round trip.
+ *
+ * Locking: every socket read/write/close and all queue/cache state lives
+ * under sock_mutex_. send() holds it across its write+timed-read so the
+ * thread can never steal a solicited reply; the thread's own IO is
+ * non-blocking (FIONREAD probe), so it never holds the lock long.
+ *
+ * Mixed versions interoperate: an unpatched peer just answers slowly (old
+ * behaviour); a patched peer answers from its thread even while the frontend
+ * has it paused.
+ */
+
+// A solicited reply normally lands in ~1 RTT (patched peer) or within a frame
+// or two (unpatched peer). 10s only fires on a dead/half-open connection —
+// where the old code blocked the emulation thread forever.
+static const int REPLY_TIMEOUT_SECS = 10;
+
+// Service thread poll cadence: bounds the extra reply latency this side adds
+// to a peer's transfer.
+static const int SERVICE_POLL_US = 1000;
 
